# ASR / Audio-LLM Domain Rules

Distilled from: Aksenova et al. 2021 "How Might We Create Better Benchmarks for Speech Recognition?" (ACL bppf), the ASR Leaderboard 2025 paper (arXiv 2510.06961), Whisper paper (Radford et al. 2023), Step-Audio 2 / Kimi-Audio / Seed-ASR / Qwen-Audio technical reports, NeurIPS 2026 evaluations & datasets call.

## 1. Reporting WER (and friends)

- Never report a single WER without specifying:
  - dataset name AND split (e.g. `LibriSpeech test-clean`, not just `LibriSpeech`)
  - normalization pipeline (e.g. "Whisper English normalizer", or describe rules: lowercase, expand contractions, strip punctuation, number normalization)
  - decoding configuration (greedy vs beam-N, temperature, length penalty, max new tokens)
  - whether you re-ran the baseline yourself or copied the number from a paper (cite both ways)
- The same normalization MUST be applied to both reference and hypothesis, and MUST be the same across compared systems. If you can't apply identical normalization across rows in a table, split the table or annotate with a footnote.
- Report at least one slice in addition to the average: per-language for multilingual, per-domain for multi-domain, per-noise-condition for robustness, U-WER vs B-WER for contextual ASR. A single average WER is necessary but never sufficient.
- If reporting CER, MER, BLEU, COMET (for translation), or named-entity F1, define the metric in a footnote at first use.

## 2. Baselines and comparisons

- For every baseline number you cite or include in a table, you must specify one of:
  - "as reported in [cite]" with the cite
  - "we re-ran with [config]" with brief reproduction details
  - "from public leaderboard [URL] retrieved on [date]"
- Strongly prefer re-running with your own normalizer for the headline table; cite-as-reported numbers belong in a separate appendix table titled "Comparison to numbers reported in prior work".
- Required baselines for an English LLM-ASR report: Whisper-large-v3 (must), at least one strong recent open model (Canary / Parakeet / OWSM v3.1 / SeamlessM4T), and your prior internal version if any.

## 3. Data section requirements

For every data source listed:

- name
- hours (audio duration after VAD filtering — state which)
- number of utterances or segments
- language(s) and language code (ISO 639-1/3)
- license (SPDX identifier if possible) and whether redistributable
- domain category (read / spontaneous / conversational / broadcast / call-center / etc.)
- collection year or release year
- citation

Add a transparent description of the cleaning pipeline:

- VAD model and threshold
- language ID model
- ASR-based confidence filter (if any), threshold, and filter rate
- de-duplication: utterance-level (e.g. MinHash threshold), and document-level
- PII / profanity scrubbing if applicable

Include a per-source table summing to a clear "Total" row.

## 4. Model / architecture description

For every component, report:

- module name (Encoder / Adapter / LLM)
- backbone identity and version (e.g. `Whisper-large-v3 encoder`, `Qwen2.5-7B-Base`)
- parameter count (millions or billions, with one decimal)
- hidden dimension, number of layers, attention heads
- frame rate (Hz) at the boundary between modules
- which weights are frozen / trainable / LoRA-adapted in each training stage

For the prompt template, paste the literal template (using `lstlisting` with the `prompt` style defined in `main.tex`). Do not paraphrase.

## 5. Training section requirements

- State the objective (e.g. token-level CE on transcript tokens, with prompt-token loss masking) explicitly with the formula on first use.
- Per stage: trainable params, learning rate schedule, batch size in audio-hours and in tokens, sequence length cap, total tokens consumed, total update steps, precision (fp32 / bf16 / fp16-amp), optimizer (AdamW betas, weight decay).
- Hardware: GPU/TPU type and count, parallelism strategy (FSDP / ZeRO-3 / TP+PP), throughput in TFLOPS or audio-hours/GPU/day, total wall-clock training time.
- If RL / DPO is used: preference data source, pair construction policy, KL coefficient.

## 6. Robustness, long-form, streaming

If the model claims any of the following, you must include the corresponding evaluation:

- "Robust to noise" → SNR sweep on a noise-augmented dev set with at least 3 SNR levels.
- "Long-form" → numbers on at least one long-form benchmark (TED-LIUM 3 long-form, GigaSpeech long, AMI, Earnings22, or a corpus of ≥30 min recordings).
- "Streaming" → measured first-token latency and real-time factor with the streaming setting actually used.
- "Multilingual" → FLEURS or similar multilingual benchmark with per-language-group averages.
- "Code-switch" → at least one benchmark like SEAME or ASCEND.

If the model does not claim a property, it is acceptable to omit the corresponding evaluation, but say so explicitly in Limitations.

## 7. Failure modes (must include)

LLM-based ASR has well-documented failure modes — be honest:

- Repetition / hallucination loops on silence or noise.
- Language drift under code-switching.
- Rare named-entity errors (especially without contextual biasing).
- Sensitivity to the system prompt.
- Catastrophic forgetting of the LLM's text capabilities after speech fine-tuning.

Show one qualitative example per claimed failure mode if making the claim.
